# {{lb|gmw-cfr|chiefly|Kölsch}} a [[cheese]] [[sandwich]], cut in half

{{C|gmw-cfr|Foods}}

==German==

===Etymology===
{{bor+|de|ksh|[[halv|halve]] [[Hahn]]|lit=half a cock/rooster}}. The origin is possibly an order like {{m+|ksh||Kann ich ne Halve hann?|Can I get half [a sandwich]?}}, the last two words of which were then re-interpreted (either by foreigners or jokingly) as ''halve Hahn''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˌhalvəˈhaːn/}}

===Noun===
{{head|de|noun|g=m}}

# {{lb|de|regional|in the Rhineland}} a [[cheese]] [[sandwich]], cut in half

====Usage notes====
* The expression is rare in actual usage. Even in thick Ripuarian dialect one generally uses the plain and simple word {{m|gmw-cfr|Kiesbrütche}} ("cheese sandwich"). However, ''Halve Hahn'' is occasionally used as a [[shibboleth]] among Rhineland people and can be found on the menus of traditional Colognian pubs (as a humorous knowledge test for foreigners).
* The term is either uninflected, e.g. (accusative) ''den Halve Hahn'', or the first part behaves like a normal German adjective, thus ''den Halve'''n''' Hahn'', and so on.

